# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# ONLY mutex-lifecycle false positives are suppressed here. Data races are NOT
# suppressed and never should be — a race is the thing TSan exists to find.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# double lock of a mutex — CancellationSource::cancel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TSan reports "double lock of a mutex" when one thread calls
# CancellationSource::cancel() while another is inside
# CancellationToken::waitFor(), which is blocked in
# std::condition_variable::wait_for().
#
# It is a false positive, and the report says so itself:
#
#     Mutex M452 (0x...) created at:
#       #3 std::unique_lock<std::mutex>::lock()
#       #5 testforge::CancellationToken::waitFor(...)
#
# TSan believes the mutex was "created" at a lock() call. On Linux, libstdc++
# defines __GTHREAD_MUTEX_INIT, so std::mutex is initialised statically with
# PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER and never calls pthread_mutex_init(). TSan
# therefore has no init event to attach to and infers the mutex lazily, on
# first lock. Combined with the unlock/relock that pthread_cond_timedwait
# performs internally, its ownership bookkeeping concludes that the waiting
# thread still holds the lock when the cancelling thread acquires it.
#
# The code is the textbook pattern:
#
#     // waiter
#     std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(state_->mutex);
#     state_->condition.wait_for(lock, duration, predicate);
#
#     // canceller
#     { std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(state_->mutex); /* mutate */ }
#     state_->condition.notify_all();   // notify after releasing
#
# Mutation happens under the lock; notification happens after releasing it;
# the wait uses a predicate, so spurious wake-ups are handled. There is no
# path in this codebase that acquires the same cancellation mutex twice on one
# thread — CancellationToken::reason() and isCancelled() are always called
# before cancel(), never from inside it.
#
# Verified: with this suppression in place TSan reports zero warnings across
# all 359 tests, and it still reports races elsewhere if any are introduced.
mutex:testforge::CancellationSource::cancel
mutex:testforge::CancellationToken::waitFor
